INT. RIVERBOAT COURTROOM - DAY - 1936

A converted paddle steamer docked at a Mississippi landing, its saloon turned into a courtroom. A chandelier sways. Through the porthole windows brown water slides past.

JUDGE PRENDERGAST HOLCOMB, 70s, white-whiskered, ancient, presides from a bench made of a bar counter. VIOLA STARLING, 30s, defence counsel in a navy suit and pearls, stands with her notes. At the prosecution table, SILAS DELACROIX, 50s, pot-bellied, in a cream suit, fans himself with a folder. On the witness stand, a rattled WITNESS, 40s, grips the rail: ODELL BANKS.

VIOLA
Mr. Banks, where were you on the night of the fourteenth?

ODELL
At the landing, ma'am.

SILAS
Objection! Leading!

JUDGE HOLCOMB
Sustained. Rephrase.

VIOLA
Where were you?

ODELL
I told you. The landing.

SILAS
He's answered!

VIOLA
He's repeated himself!

JUDGE HOLCOMB
Order, both of you!

The steamer lurches on its moorings. Silas grabs the table. Viola steadies herself on the witness rail. Odell squeaks. The judge's gavel rolls off the bar counter and clatters to the floor.

SILAS
(to the judge)
Your Honour, the court is moving!

JUDGE HOLCOMB
The court is a boat, Mr. Delacroix.

VIOLA
(over him)
Mr. Banks, did you see the defendant at the landing?

ODELL
I saw somebody.

SILAS
Hearsay!

JUDGE HOLCOMB
Overruled. I've been seasick since nine.

The room erupts in laughter. Silas turns crimson. The chandelier swings. Viola presses a handkerchief to her lips, hiding a smile.
